        "readable_text": "Content:\nHere, we'll derive the formula\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1).]\n\nContent:\nFirst, let's represent the sum with the letter [MATH: S.] This way, it will be easier to manipulate the sum algebraically.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = \\sum_{n=1}^N n]\n\nContent:\nNext, let's write out [MATH: S] twice. The first time, we'll write the numbers in ascending order, and the second time in descending order:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = 1|+ 2 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1) + N; S = N|+ (N \u2212 1) + \u22ef + 2 + 1]\n\nContent:\nNow, if we add the two sums above, we get the following:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2S|= (N + 1) + (2 + N \u2212 1) + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1 + 2) + (N + 1); = (N + 1) + (N + 1) + \u22ef + (N + 1) + (N + 1)_(\u23df)_(Ntimes); = N(N + 1)]\n\nContent:\nFinally, solving for [MATH: S], we reach the desired result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)]"
      }
