    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nIf we wish to sum the first [MATH: N] integers, an easy way is to use the following formula:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n|= 1 + 2 + 3 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1) + N; = (1)/(2)N(N + 1)]\n\nContent:\nAs we will see, we can use this simple formula to evaluate any series of the form [MATH: \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^N (an+b)}.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of the First N Integers\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nUsing the formula\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n|= (1)/(2)N(N + 1)]\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n|= (1)/(2) \u22c5 100 \u22c5 (100 + 1); = (1)/(2) \u22c5 100 \u22c5 101; = 5050.]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(50)n =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 2275]\nb. [MATH: 1255]\nc. [MATH: 1245]\nd. [MATH: 1275]\ne. [MATH: 1075]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(30)n.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 490]\nb. [MATH: 870]\nc. [MATH: 435]\nd. [MATH: 930]\ne. [MATH: 465]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Finite Linear Series with a Constant Factor\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nFirst, we take the factor [MATH: 4] outside the summation:\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^{100} 4n = 4\\sum_{n=1}^{100} n]\n\nNow, using the formula\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(N)n|= (1)/(2)N(N + 1)]\nwith [MATH: N=100,] we get\n\n[MATH: 4\u2211_(n = 1)^(100)n|= 4((1)/(2) \u22c5 100 \u22c5 101); = 4 \u22c5 5050; = 20200.]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Question 3\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(120)(n)/(2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 7280]\nb. [MATH: 3630]\nc. [MATH: 3430]\nd. [MATH: 7260]\ne. [MATH: 3670]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(100)6n.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 30500]\nb. [MATH: 29400]\nc. [MATH: 30350]\nd. [MATH: 30300]\ne. [MATH: 31300]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Example: Calculating the Sum of a Finite Linear Series with a Constant Term\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: \\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nWe can write the summation as follows:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(200)(3n + 2)|= \u2211_(n = 1)^(200)3n + \u2211_(n = 1)^(200)2; = 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(200)n + 2\u2211_(n = 1)^(200)1]\n\nLet's consider the two series separately.\n\n- For the first series, we use the formula\n[MATH: \\displaystyle \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)]\nwith [MATH: N=200,] and we get\n[MATH: 3\u2211_(n = 1)^(200)n|= 3((1)/(2) \u22c5 200 \u22c5 201); = 3 \u22c5 20100; = 60300.]\n- In the second series, [MATH: \\displaystyle{\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1}] is just [MATH: 1] added together [MATH: 200] times, which is [MATH: 200 \\cdot 1 = 200.] So, we have\n[MATH: 2\\sum_{n=1}^{200} 1 =2 \\cdot 200 = 400.]\n\nFinally, we have\n\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^{200} (3n+2) = 60\\,300 + 400 = 60\\,700.]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 5\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\nCalculate\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(90)(8n \u2212 (1)/(4)).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 32760]\nb. [MATH: 32737]\nc. [MATH: 32782.5]\nd. [MATH: 32737.5]\ne. [MATH: 32760.5]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Question 6\n\nCalculator Instructions:\nA scientific calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: \u2211_(n = 1)^(150)(4n \u2212 2) =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 46000]\nb. [MATH: 45000]\nc. [MATH: 49000]\nd. [MATH: 47000]\ne. [MATH: 48000]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Tutorial: Derivation of the Formula\n\nContent:\nHere, we'll derive the formula\n\nContent:\n[MATH: \\sum_{n=1}^N n = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1).]\n\nContent:\nFirst, let's represent the sum with the letter [MATH: S.] This way, it will be easier to manipulate the sum algebraically.\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = \\sum_{n=1}^N n]\n\nContent:\nNext, let's write out [MATH: S] twice. The first time, we'll write the numbers in ascending order, and the second time in descending order:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = 1|+ 2 + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1) + N; S = N|+ (N \u2212 1) + \u22ef + 2 + 1]\n\nContent:\nNow, if we add the two sums above, we get the following:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: 2S|= (N + 1) + (2 + N \u2212 1) + \u22ef + (N \u2212 1 + 2) + (N + 1); = (N + 1) + (N + 1) + \u22ef + (N + 1) + (N + 1)_(\u23df)_(Ntimes); = N(N + 1)]\n\nContent:\nFinally, solving for [MATH: S], we reach the desired result:\n\nContent:\n[MATH: S = \\dfrac 1 2 N (N+1)]"
